1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.800
Tim Hawks:

2
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:14.305
Two sessions left after today and you guys are the faithful ones hanging in there till the end. Congratulations. This is exciting. Can we recite our definition of what it means to be a man?

3
00:00:14.305 --> 00:00:23.808
A real man is somebody who rejects passivity, accepts responsibility, leads courageously, and expects a greater reward, God's reward.

4
00:00:23.808 --> 00:00:34.597
Now, men in the world typically get a bad rap from time to time. And one of those places where we get a bad rap is when it comes to driving directions.

5
00:00:34.597 --> 00:00:50.174
You know, the women have said for years and years that men will not stop for directions, and they have got it all wrong. It is not that we do not want to know where we are going. It is that we just do not want to stop.

6
00:00:50.174 --> 00:00:58.248
And so, I mean, eventually, if you keep going, you are going to get someplace. And someplace is better than here.

7
00:00:58.248 --> 00:01:09.323
And so, my wife has never figured out why if the gas station or the McDonald's is not on the right hand side of the highway, we are just not going to stop.

8
00:01:09.323 --> 00:01:23.185
Because if you have to cross over the highway, you have to make multiple stops to get back to your destination. So, we are just not going to do it. It is not about lack of desire for directions.

9
00:01:23.185 --> 00:01:28.758
It is once you reject passivity and you start moving, you do not want to stop.

10
00:01:28.758 --> 00:01:43.406
And so, today we are going to talk about a man and his life journey. Let me just suggest that the younger you are in the room, the less relevant this will feel to you and the most helpful it will be. Okay?

11
00:01:43.406 --> 00:01:56.196
So, young people often project in their lives that they have got this thing figured out and the more insight that you take in now, the better you are going to be in the long term.

12
00:01:56.196 --> 00:02:08.629
And so what we are going to do today is we are going to dive in and we are going to look at two different approaches to life cycle, life stage, like what does that look like?

13
00:02:08.629 --> 00:02:15.202
We are building this off of two books and the research that had come out of those two books.

14
00:02:15.202 --> 00:02:27.921
And the first one is really a life cycle map. It was developed by Daniel Levinson in, it was actually published in 1978. You say that was a long time ago. It was a long time ago.

15
00:02:27.921 --> 00:02:38.782
I have kind of gone back and looked at this model every time I have taught this study over the last 20 plus years, and I still think the model is valid.

16
00:02:38.782 --> 00:02:51.143
Now, what they did was they followed 40 men around at different stages of their life for a 10-year period and studied these men to understand kind of what do men go through.

17
00:02:51.143 --> 00:03:00.646
So, again, finding sociological studies that have that kind of longitude are also difficult to do. So, we are going to look at these.

18
00:03:00.646 --> 00:03:14.151
And I just want to give you a quick overview on the stair steps. At the first stair step, 0 to 22. Above the stair step, write spring. 17 to 45, write summer. Going up 40 to 65, write fall.

19
00:03:14.151 --> 00:03:27.870
And then above 60, write winter. Now, going down below on the line below that describes each of these stages, let me give you the descriptor. So 0 to 22 the spring is childhood to adolescence.

20
00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:37.802
The summer 17 to 45 write early adulthood. The fall ages 40 to 65 write middle adulthood. And then the winter 60 plus write late adulthood.

21
00:03:37.802 --> 00:03:57.452
Now, what I want to do is walk through these reasonably quickly and describe each of these phases of life, kind of the general description and talk about the transitions because one of the most important things as a man are these transitions where men do a bit of evaluation.

22
00:03:57.452 --> 00:04:10.671
You stop and look at the map. You stop and wrestle with where am I at? And what you decide to do in these transitions will end up becoming what shapes your life, what moves you forward.

23
00:04:10.671 --> 00:04:22.032
And so looking at the first stage, the spring stage 0 to 22, the adolescent stage, there is a question that you have to ask and answer in each of these stages.

24
00:04:22.032 --> 00:04:32.321
And the first question that you answer in your childhood and adolescence is who am I? It is an identity issue. A child is growing up physically.

25
00:04:32.321 --> 00:04:45.254
And so as your physical development takes place, your characteristics, your brain, how you are coming together, what your personality is, you are trying to identify who am I really.

26
00:04:45.254 --> 00:04:53.042
And there is also an environmental factor. So it is not just the physical factors, the environmental factors.

27
00:04:53.042 --> 00:05:07.261
My family, the place I grow up in, the background I am raised in, my spiritual roots, all of those begin to shape a young man, a child into an understanding of who they are and who they are becoming.

28
00:05:07.261 --> 00:05:16.264
Now, we will talk a little bit more about this in the next model, but I want to get that to you. The transition at this stage.

29
00:05:16.264 --> 00:05:31.627
His model was 22, age 22, kind of the top end of it, and then summer 17 to 45 when you see that transition, that inflection point. You say, well, they overlap. It is because there is a period in the transition here.

30
00:05:31.627 --> 00:05:41.630
I would change these numbers. I would change 22 to 25 for the childhood stage. And I would change the summer from 21 to 45. And here is why.

31
00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:52.777
We are experiencing a significant period of delayed adulthood in our world today where kids stay kids longer and boys stay children longer than in the past.

32
00:05:52.777 --> 00:06:08.140
It is interesting that if you look at the market for video games today, watch the advertisements for video games, you do not see teenagers in the videos. You see young adults in the video, some of them 30 years old.

33
00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:16.500
You just see that the marketing is not to children anymore. They have got the childhood market. That is not an issue.

34
00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:25.074
They are continuing to market to people that are playing games some significant periods of time up into their adulthood.

35
00:06:25.074 --> 00:06:32.505
And so there is this period that takes place that takes a long time for kids to transition to adulthood.

36
00:06:32.505 --> 00:06:47.725
And the central issue that makes that change in a young boy turning into a man is this. When it dawns on this young adult that I cannot keep on going like this for the rest of my life, something has got to change.

37
00:06:47.725 --> 00:06:59.372
If that happens at 17, adulthood starts. If that happens at 21, adulthood starts. If that happens at 25, adulthood starts. If that happens at 35, adulthood starts.

38
00:06:59.372 --> 00:07:12.091
And one of the key triggers here has something to do with money. As long as mom and dad continue to bankroll my lifestyle, oftentimes that question does not come home to a child.

39
00:07:12.091 --> 00:07:34.814
And since most parents have figured out a system that between their investment and the child's heavily borrowing money to pay for college, many young men graduate from college and actually think somehow that this is the way life works because they have personally invested very little in work up to that point in time.

40
00:07:34.814 --> 00:07:41.030
They have invested very little in earning their way through life at that point in time.

41
00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:56.535
And somehow in the same way that in our constitution, we have written into the inalienable rights things like health care, we have also written in a four-year college degree paid for by parents, government, and loans.

42
00:07:56.535 --> 00:08:09.254
And we have written those in. And so along the way, it is hard to own this thinking, this feeling that like life cannot go on like this. I cannot keep living off of other people.

43
00:08:09.254 --> 00:08:12.255
I need to take responsibility for my life.

44
00:08:12.255 --> 00:08:27.546
And so, dads, it is important that from an early age, you start creating opportunities for your children to work, to earn. You start talking to them along the way about finances. What am I going to provide for you?

45
00:08:27.546 --> 00:08:42.480
What am I not going to provide for you? You start talking to your kids about college based on their merit. In other words, what have they done to earn scholarship money and what school should they be going to?

46
00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:51.269
And you are not going to underwrite an education that you and they cannot afford to go to. You are just not going to do it.

47
00:08:51.269 --> 00:09:11.133
And so just because your parents paid your full ride to a private college, which by the way when you went through that private college cost a fraction of what the inflated college costs have gone up over the past 10 years since the government took over the student loan program.

48
00:09:11.133 --> 00:09:26.424
You guys realize that? Lower the interest rates. When they did that, colleges just jacked up their cost. You all realize that, right? Living the reality of what is actually happening in our country. It helps a lot.

49
00:09:26.424 --> 00:09:32.426
I hear people being interviewed all the time say, well, the economy is not that bad.

50
00:09:32.426 --> 00:09:44.216
Well, it is not, except we have a liability of $21 trillion that we have committed to and a hundred million of unfunded liabilities that we are going to have to pay.

51
00:09:44.216 --> 00:09:59.721
And like, the economy is not bad as long as we keep borrowing money. But your kids, there is going to come a day when your kids are 45 and that devalues the currency. Everybody's 401k is worth half what it was before.

52
00:09:59.721 --> 00:10:10.510
Like wow, what is wrong with us? What is wrong with us? It is because we are not thinking. We are not living that way. So you got to begin the process.

53
00:10:10.510 --> 00:10:24.015
Because if you wait till your son is 25, you have never had a conversation. All of a sudden you realize this is not helping. And I got to cut him off. That becomes a real hard conversation.

54
00:10:24.015 --> 00:10:36.305
Okay, so that is a huge huge issue to begin with your boys to teach them ownership. You cannot keep living like this because it is not good for you. It is not good for you.

55
00:10:36.305 --> 00:10:52.025
Moving on to the next stage, the summer, early adulthood, somewhere between 21 and 45. During this phase, you are asking the question, what will I do? What will I do with my life? What am I going to be as far as my work?

56
00:10:52.025 --> 00:11:02.171
What am I going to do with my time? Am I going to get married? Am I going to raise a family? Those kinds of questions are coming up this time.

57
00:11:02.171 --> 00:11:10.817
And generally speaking, there is crisis somewhere along the way in this when you get out, you get started, you get going.

58
00:11:10.817 --> 00:11:21.106
And by experience, you may find out that what you have chosen and what you are embracing, you really do not fit with and you really do not like.

59
00:11:21.106 --> 00:11:34.539
And a lot of guys find that a few years into their career and begin to go, oh man, I got this degree. I took this job. I hate this. Now, if you hate it, because work is a four-letter word.

60
00:11:34.539 --> 00:11:49.616
If you just hate to work, that is a different question. But if you realize that every day you get up and you are working against your wiring, your skills, your gifting and all that, that creates quite a dilemma.

61
00:11:49.616 --> 00:11:55.189
And so Jay talked about that a little bit last week with a man and his design.

62
00:11:55.189 --> 00:12:01.762
Now, the ability to make this transition is exclusively related to your appetite for things.

63
00:12:01.762 --> 00:12:21.198
And so if you have started out and you get that first job and you go out and you mortgage your life, like you take on all kinds of debt or you marry a woman who has expectations for all kinds of upscale lifestyle, oftentimes you get stuck like I have to keep grinding out.

64
00:12:21.198 --> 00:12:33.559
I cannot look for a transition. I cannot pick up some more training and education so I can move to something else. Just cannot do that because I have got these bills to pay.

65
00:12:33.559 --> 00:12:48.493
The decision along the way in this stage of life will make a major decision when it comes to the next transition because the transition between summer and fall oftentimes is when men face their midlife crisis.

66
00:12:48.493 --> 00:12:58.496
When men face their midlife crisis because entering into the next stage, the fall stage 40 to 65, the big question is, is that all there is?

67
00:12:58.496 --> 00:13:11.000
This becomes a time of introspection where you are asking the question, okay, I have got this woman I have been married to. I am raising these kids. The cycle of life goes on.

68
00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:21.789
I get up in the morning, I go to work, I come back, we go to kids activities. I get up in the morning, go to work, we come back, go to kids activities.

69
00:13:21.789 --> 00:13:37.294
And you start asking yourself the question like, is that all there is? Now I have gotten a nicer house. I have upgraded. I am driving a better car, the car that I thought I wanted to drive, and it does not satisfy me.

70
00:13:37.294 --> 00:13:39.008
I am not any more happy.

71
00:13:39.008 --> 00:13:53.084
You are starting to wrestle with the question like from this point forward, do I just continue to acquire, continue to go through the paces of my life and continue to get older and then I just die?

72
00:13:53.084 --> 00:14:03.230
Is that what is going on? And at that point, a lot of introspection comes on a man. Real deep introspection, evaluating, is that all there is?

73
00:14:03.230 --> 00:14:15.877
Typically, men do two things at this point in time. Either they blame their circumstances. This woman that I have, she does not make me happy. I am going to go find another one.

74
00:14:15.877 --> 00:14:30.954
This career that I have, it is not satisfying me. I am going to dump everything and go find another one. You know, I am going to get the dinghy and sail the world or something like that. Blame the circumstances.

75
00:14:30.954 --> 00:14:38.171
Or the positive response to that is to ask the question, have I actually seized my masculine calling?

76
00:14:38.171 --> 00:14:53.748
Do I understand the spiritual and relational dimensions of my life that are more important and more significant to me than just getting ahead, getting established, building my life? Is there something deeper than that?

77
00:14:53.748 --> 00:15:00.893
And during this phase of life, men wrestle with this and make significant progress or do not at all.

78
00:15:00.893 --> 00:15:16.613
Some of y'all who are approaching this phase of life, you perceive yourself not to be very relationally sensitive. What you are going to discover is that during this phase of life, men become very relationally sensitive.

79
00:15:16.613 --> 00:15:23.044
Very relationally sensitive. How your children view you will become very important to you.

80
00:15:23.044 --> 00:15:33.833
Whether you have a good relationship with your wife or not will become way more important to you at this phase of life than any phase up to this point.

81
00:15:33.833 --> 00:15:39.835
And you begin to change and you begin to grow and you begin to ask better questions.

82
00:15:39.835 --> 00:15:57.556
And at this point in life, you can go much deeper into what it means to be a man, what it means to be made in the image of God, what it means to be able to contribute beyond just a breadwinner or a worker or somebody who is driving the kids around.

83
00:15:57.556 --> 00:16:00.199
And this is a very significant stage.

84
00:16:00.199 --> 00:16:09.774
The last stage, the winter, you are asking the question, what do I do with the rest of my life? What do I do with the rest of my life?

85
00:16:09.774 --> 00:16:22.993
What you begin to find is that in a youth-oriented culture, you get moved off of center stage. You are no longer the young whiz kid that came in with all the answers and all the energy.

86
00:16:22.993 --> 00:16:27.566
And you start seeing people look at you in a very different way.

87
00:16:27.566 --> 00:16:42.786
You see a woman in her 30s and you think she is really attractive and she looks at you and she says, hey, grandpa, and you do not, I mean it is kind of jarring at first. It is kind of like, when did I become that?

88
00:16:42.786 --> 00:16:54.504
But that is who you are and you are now looked at as an older person and in our culture older people are not looked at as being able to make much of a contribution.

89
00:16:54.504 --> 00:17:09.366
Part of that comes down to whether you at that moment decide to elevate your game. Like, am I going to take my game up or am I going to descend into selfishness? Okay, I have worked hard. I have done my time.

90
00:17:09.366 --> 00:17:22.370
Now it is my time. Let me spend the rest of my life picking up seashells. Improving my golf score. Having the perfectly manicured lawn. Watching television for hours and hours a day.

91
00:17:22.370 --> 00:17:34.588
And what you decide to do at this point in time will make a huge difference on what we will talk about next week with your legacy, where you are going, what you are doing.

92
00:17:34.588 --> 00:17:45.878
This is a time of unbelievable contribution but it is not the same kind of contribution. It is not necessarily inventing something or starting a new business.

93
00:17:45.878 --> 00:17:53.666
Oftentimes in this part of time you are actually investing relationally, mentoring people, developing people.

94
00:17:53.666 --> 00:18:08.528
Men oftentimes at this point have more time they have more financial resources they have freedom and you either use those resources to make a huge impact or you use those resources to get focused on yourself.

95
00:18:08.528 --> 00:18:21.818
What you decide to do in this phase has a lot to do with two things. One is your impact and two is your length of life because the statistics on retirement and death are not very pretty.

96
00:18:21.818 --> 00:18:29.035
Many many guys die within a couple years of retirement because they do not have a plan to keep going.

97
00:18:29.035 --> 00:18:41.253
If you do not have a plan to keep going like as a man at the point in time when you are just completely worthless things take over and the drag becomes really significant.

98
00:18:41.253 --> 00:18:44.897
So that is looking at these various phases of life.

99
00:18:44.897 --> 00:18:53.686
Let us look at a life stage map. Now the life stage map was developed by a theologian Robert Hicks. I knew Bob in seminary.

100
00:18:53.686 --> 00:19:01.403
He was one of my professors and actually did my premarital counseling, Cindy's and my premarital counseling.

101
00:19:01.403 --> 00:19:20.767
He wrote a book based on the scriptures called *The Masculine Journey* and he looked at all the words for man or men or used of men to describe men in the Old Testament and came up with these phases of a man's life based on how those words were used, the context of them.

102
00:19:20.767 --> 00:19:34.057
Robert Lewis, the man who developed this study, this whole study originally, went in and took time frames and attached them to his categories. And so that is where this chart comes from.

103
00:19:34.057 --> 00:19:46.847
So if you will start in the very middle where you see Adam or the Hebrew word Adam, that is the creation stage. The creation stage and that is 0 to 20. The creation stage 0 to 20.

104
00:19:46.847 --> 00:20:01.781
Moving out in the concentric circles, the zakar stage is the phallic stage. That is the phallic stage. That is 13 to 25. Moving out from there, the gibbor stage is the warrior stage. That is the warrior stage.

105
00:20:01.781 --> 00:20:15.214
And that is 20 to 40. The warrior stage 20 to 40. Moving out is the enosh stage. That is what we call the wounded stage, the wounded stage. And that is 40 to 50. You guys keeping up? Okay.

106
00:20:15.214 --> 00:20:30.505
The next stage out is the ish stage. That is mature. That is the mature stage. And that is 50 to 60. And then moving out one more to the very end is the zaken stage. And we call this the sage stage, the sage stage.

107
00:20:30.505 --> 00:20:31.934
And that is 60 plus.

108
00:20:31.934 --> 00:20:46.010
So I want to walk through each of these stages. And here is what I want you to see. In each of these life stages, at each stage along the way, there is a challenge to that particular stage of life.

109
00:20:46.010 --> 00:21:00.372
Comes out of these Hebrew concepts. There is a challenge that a man faces. If you succeed in overcoming that challenge, you move into the next stage to face your new challenge. Face your new challenge.

110
00:21:00.372 --> 00:21:14.377
If you do not succeed in the former challenge, you still move into the next stage. You just carry the old challenge with you. So now you got two things that are working that you have to deal with.

111
00:21:14.377 --> 00:21:27.739
If you do not deal with either of those and you go into the third stage, now you have got three challenges that you need to deal with. And eventually the challenges will crater your life.

112
00:21:27.739 --> 00:21:38.886
Now when we go through this, some of you guys are going to go, oh yeah, I still struggle with this and that should have been dealt with a couple stages ago.

113
00:21:38.886 --> 00:21:47.818
That is why it is such a hard deal because I am facing the pressures here, but I got this baggage that I am bringing with me.

114
00:21:47.818 --> 00:22:03.038
And so, as you look at this model, that is part of what we are trying to identify is at each stage, we need to deal with the new presenting challenge to be able to move to the next stage and not carry him with us.

115
00:22:03.038 --> 00:22:18.615
So, the first stage, the creational stage, the Adam stage is the first challenge 0 to 20. In that stage, we are wrestling with a question, will I accept who God created me to be? Will I accept who God created me to be?

116
00:22:18.615 --> 00:22:32.906
Now, in Psalm 139 verses 13-16, David writes these words. He says, "For you created my inmost being. You knit me together in my mother's womb. I praise you because I am fearfully and wonderfully made.

117
00:22:32.906 --> 00:22:47.483
Your works are wonderful. I know that full well. My frame was not hidden from you when I was made in the secret place. When I was woven together in the depths of the earth, your eyes saw my unformed body.

118
00:22:47.483 --> 00:22:55.271
All the days ordained for me were written in your book before one of them came to be." What is he describing?

119
00:22:55.271 --> 00:23:09.419
He is describing God's involvement in shaping you from the time you were in the womb to have the characteristics that you have, the physical, the emotional, the mental characteristics that you have.

120
00:23:09.419 --> 00:23:21.852
And then from a spiritual standpoint in Ephesians chapter 2 verse 10, Paul writes, "For we are God's workmanship." We are God's workmanship. God did a work in us spiritually.

121
00:23:21.852 --> 00:23:31.712
So we have spiritual attributes that shape who we are created in Christ Jesus to do good works which God prepared in advance for us to do.

122
00:23:31.712 --> 00:23:46.717
So all of us as we start coming into ourselves begin to gain self-awareness. We are four, we are five, we are six, we are seven, we are eight. Our personality is taking shape. Our physical form is taking shape.

123
00:23:46.717 --> 00:24:02.580
We are wrestling with, do I want to accept and develop who God created me to be or am I going to be confused about this and struggle with this and fight against this identity and want to be somebody that I am actually not.

124
00:24:02.580 --> 00:24:16.728
And we all face this, right? I remember as a kid growing up, I love basketball. That was kind of my, that was the love of my life. I love basketball. And so as a kid growing up, I wanted to be 6'6".

125
00:24:16.728 --> 00:24:28.303
And I just remember standing against the wall where we measured and doing everything I could to try to get that height. And you know, obviously I did not make it.

126
00:24:28.303 --> 00:24:42.880
So when my youngest son Sam was in middle school, he was so developmentally behind. He went to Westwood High School as a freshman. Did not have a hair on his body except on his head. I mean, he was a kid.

127
00:24:42.880 --> 00:24:57.171
And our older son said, "Dad, he is going to get killed in the locker room. Those kids will just ruin him in the locker room." So anyway, we were wondering like does he have some developmental issues?

128
00:24:57.171 --> 00:25:12.748
So we took him to the doctor and she did all the tests and checked him out, his hormones and all that. And she said to us, she said, sure enough, he is way behind developmentally, but the good news is he will catch up.

129
00:25:12.748 --> 00:25:24.538
In fact, he will catch up and he will pass all the other kids at some point in his future. And she said, in fact, he is going to be, he is probably going to be 6'6".

130
00:25:24.538 --> 00:25:41.758
So, I went home and told Sam, I said, hey, I know you feel like you are really small compared to all these other kids and they are getting muscles and hair and you are not, but you are going to catch up and you are going to go ahead of them.

131
00:25:41.758 --> 00:25:57.192
In fact, the doctor said, you are going to be 6'6". He went, oh, no. You have got to be kidding me. I am going to be tall and I am going to be awkward and I am going to be lanky and that is just going to be terrible.

132
00:25:57.192 --> 00:26:12.912
So, I want to be 6'6". He did not want to be 6'6". Well, now that you can dunk a basketball, it is a little bit more fun than it was when he first thought that. But it is always interesting. Short people want to be tall.

133
00:26:12.912 --> 00:26:22.272
Tall people want to be muscular. Muscular people want to be smart. Smart people want to be funny. Funny people want to be artistic.

134
00:26:22.272 --> 00:26:29.989
Like everybody has a tendency to look at a characteristic in someone else and go, that is what I want to be.

135
00:26:29.989 --> 00:26:42.922
Here is what happens at this stage. At this stage, you either accept who you are. Like, I am kind of slow. I am kind of dumpy, but man, I can organize things. I can organize things.

136
00:26:42.922 --> 00:26:52.925
Or like, I will never win a beauty contest, but man, I have got the ability to read people. I can read people when other people miss things.

137
00:26:52.925 --> 00:27:02.571
So along the way, it is deciding the way God made me with my physical body, with my mental acumen, with my sensitivities and my wiring.

138
00:27:02.571 --> 00:27:12.789
Will I accept that and build on that and build my life around that or am I going to continue to struggle wishing I was something that I am not.

139
00:27:12.789 --> 00:27:28.152
And so for parents during this stage, you want to help your kids accept who they are by asking a few questions. One of those is what does it mean to be me? Like what does it mean to be me? And what is good about it?

140
00:27:28.152 --> 00:27:40.799
What are the strengths in it? Second question is how should it be used? Like what are the applications of my wiring and my abilities? What are the positive things that I can do?

141
00:27:40.799 --> 00:27:55.376
It is really important that you do not tell your kids the lie that they can be anything they want to be because that is just not true. I have got a daughter that played college volleyball at the D1 level.

142
00:27:55.376 --> 00:28:06.380
She has got a wingspan and a 6'2" almost 6'3" frame. And she will never never never never never be able to dunk a basketball at the height of her working.

143
00:28:06.380 --> 00:28:18.241
She could put her hand on the rim, but for those of you guys who can dunk one, you know you got to get about three or four inches higher than that to be able to dunk.

144
00:28:18.241 --> 00:28:23.314
So when she was in the best shape of her life, she could never do that.

145
00:28:23.314 --> 00:28:37.033
Now, as a dad, if I kept telling her, you can do anything you want to, she spends the rest of her life thinking, what is wrong with me that I am not playing in the WNBA. What is wrong with me?

146
00:28:37.033 --> 00:28:41.820
It is nothing wrong with you. God did not make you to play in that.

147
00:28:41.820 --> 00:28:53.467
And so, it is very important that you start helping your kids understand how great it is to be them and how unique their gifting is and why that is so significant.

148
00:28:53.467 --> 00:29:09.187
Because we have lots and lots of millennials that are very disappointed in their life because they want to be Mark Zuckerberg and they do not understand why they cannot. And so they change three or four times in college.

149
00:29:09.187 --> 00:29:22.263
They change their majors. They get out. They cannot figure out what to do because they have been told all their life, you can do whatever you want to do. Well, not really. You cannot.

150
00:29:22.263 --> 00:29:36.982
It is better to embrace what God has made you to be and love it and grow in it than it is to pretend something that just is not accurate. The last question is for whom? So, what does it mean to be who I am?

151
00:29:36.982 --> 00:29:51.416
How should it be used? And then for whom who am I going to use this? And that is probably the most significant question. Am I using it for myself? Am I using it for my boss? Am I using it to get a girl?

152
00:29:51.416 --> 00:29:54.631
Am I using it for God? Am I using it for God?

153
00:29:54.631 --> 00:30:03.491
Second stage is the phallic stage. The zakar stage. The Hebrew word zakar means protrusion and it is used of the male penis.

154
00:30:03.491 --> 00:30:10.636
During this stage, starting at age 13, men start experiencing a heightened sense of sexual identity.

155
00:30:10.636 --> 00:30:23.426
They begin to identify that there is a strong sex drive that is chemical and there is development that goes along with that. And between ages 13 and 25, it is at the highest peak.

156
00:30:23.426 --> 00:30:37.145
Now, you can grow up believing the cultural lie that you cannot control that. That is just not true. That is what Satan would have you believe. That is what the culture would have you believe.

157
00:30:37.145 --> 00:30:48.577
Part of the reason why the culture would have you believe that is because a major portion of our marketing is based on sex. Sex sells provocative sexual things.

158
00:30:48.577 --> 00:30:57.937
And so if we had a celibate, self-controlled masculinity, it would change a lot of what is being promoted out there in the culture.

159
00:30:57.937 --> 00:31:09.298
But once we say all men are just dogs, you cannot control it, then the culture feels free to promote it at every level. So this is a big deal to get a hold of.

160
00:31:09.298 --> 00:31:19.444
If you do not deal with who you are, you carry that into the next phase. So I am not comfortable in who I am and I have got this sexual drive.

161
00:31:19.444 --> 00:31:34.807
Now a child growing into adulthood has two big issues to really wrestle with at the time. The challenge here is will I master my sexual drive or will it master me? Will I master my sexual drive or will it master me?

162
00:31:34.807 --> 00:31:48.097
Will I be able to control it and channel it into the positive place that God designed it for? Or will I let it control me and let it run me into all kinds of places that I should not be.

163
00:31:48.097 --> 00:32:02.745
Bob Hicks, the guy who developed the format for this, he said, I have seen men in their 50s and 60s who still think of life as below their belt most of the time. They have never moved on in their maleness.

164
00:32:02.745 --> 00:32:14.606
They have never learned that sexual energy must be channeled constructively. One of the biggest challenges for young people today is the proliferation of pornography.

165
00:32:14.606 --> 00:32:33.899
In fact, I interacted with a guy who was doing a major study on this and they were trying to find test people who had been looking at pornography every day versus young people that had not and they could not find any in the second group and they just could not find any.

166
00:32:33.899 --> 00:32:48.761
I mean the exposure is just over the top. Starting to see erectile dysfunction with men in their 30s now. And it has nothing to do with a physiological thing. So, you cannot give them Viagra to help them out.

167
00:32:48.761 --> 00:33:07.268
It is because they have been so stimulated by pornography since they were early teens that they have actually gotten to a point where their brain does not work anymore, where unless they have extreme forms of pornography, they cannot even get aroused anymore.

168
00:33:07.268 --> 00:33:21.845
I sent that to my oldest son and he sent me back something that was really interesting. He sent me back a Reddit chat forum that has 95,000 male members who are talking about kicking their porn addiction.

169
00:33:21.845 --> 00:33:25.775
They are not coming at it from a moralistic standpoint.

170
00:33:25.775 --> 00:33:40.637
They are talking about the 8, 10, 12 years of their life where they viewed pornography and masturbated every day of their life and it has just ruined their relationships, their ability to connect with people.

171
00:33:40.637 --> 00:33:48.997
And these are secular, many of them secular guys who are trying to support each other in breaking the porn addiction.

172
00:33:48.997 --> 00:34:03.645
And you just get on there and you read the stories of guys that after 60 or 90 days of not viewing porn are experiencing a total change in the way their mind looks at the world, how they relate to females.

173
00:34:03.645 --> 00:34:14.220
I mean just total change. So because pornography stimulates chemicals in the body, it has the same kind of addictive property that using drugs does.

174
00:34:14.220 --> 00:34:22.723
The difference is once your high is over, it is over and your body goes into withdrawal and you have to deal with that.

175
00:34:22.723 --> 00:34:39.086
When you are using pornography, since your mind can bring those images back up at any time, placing images in your mind means that that chemical reaction can start when you are sitting in a business meeting having a conversation.

176
00:34:39.086 --> 00:34:53.948
And that is why so many guys run back to their computer because the chemical thing started and they run back to their computer in their office and end up getting caught doing porn at work and lose their jobs.

177
00:34:53.948 --> 00:35:07.881
You say, that is just so stupid. Why would a guy do that? Well, because it is an addiction. And so, helping our kids stay away from this and us not leading them into this is very, very important.

178
00:35:07.881 --> 00:35:23.244
In 1 Thessalonians 4:3 through 8, listen to what Paul says. This is very important. He says, "It is God's will that you should be sanctified." So, God's will that you should be sanctified. What does that mean, Paul?

179
00:35:23.244 --> 00:35:27.388
He goes on to say that you should avoid sexual immorality.

180
00:35:27.388 --> 00:35:39.106
That each of you should, watch this, learn to control his own body in a way that is holy and honorable, not in passionate lust like the heathen who do not know God.

181
00:35:39.106 --> 00:35:50.610
And that in this matter, no one should wrong his brother or take advantage of him. The Lord will punish men for such sins as we have already told and warned you.

182
00:35:50.610 --> 00:36:06.258
For God did not call us to be impure, but to live a holy life. Therefore, he who rejects this instruction does not reject man, but God, who gives you his Holy Spirit. We are called to a life of holiness to control this.

183
00:36:06.258 --> 00:36:31.624
I would just say guys, if you find yourself at any age struggling with this sexual drive, if you have carried this into your 40s, if you have carried it into your 50s, the sexual drive that you are satisfying outside of a love relationship with your spouse, I would challenge you, do not keep carrying this because you will carry it the rest of your life.

184
00:36:31.624 --> 00:36:49.488
I had one of my seminary professors told me that he was praying with a guy and the guy was in his 80s and they knelt down to pray and they started to pray and the guy in his 80s said God I pray that you would deliver me from becoming a dirty old man.

185
00:36:49.488 --> 00:36:59.992
He is praying that prayer in his 80s. He said I did not think people in their 80s could even have those thoughts. You can carry it your whole life.

186
00:36:59.992 --> 00:37:14.926
So, if you think that what you are struggling with will go away naturally, 28-year-old guy, 32-year-old guy, 40-year-old guy, if it had not gone away yet, you need to take aggressive means to make that change.

187
00:37:14.926 --> 00:37:29.002
Put filters on your computer, DVR the game, and zap all the commercials so you do not have to watch the Victoria's Secret model bouncing around. Because for some people that just triggers it again.

188
00:37:29.002 --> 00:37:39.291
Celebrate Recovery on Monday nights. Get in there. Take active aggressive steps to deal with this issue. Because if you do not, you carry it on.

189
00:37:39.291 --> 00:37:53.868
Now, you can see if you are carrying on your identity like from your 20s, you do not deal with who God made me to be and I am happy with myself. I am excited about that. And you are carrying that forward.

190
00:37:53.868 --> 00:38:06.658
And then you cannot deal with your sexuality. It is controlling you. And you carry that forward. Think about what the next phase of your life is carrying those two issues forward.

191
00:38:06.658 --> 00:38:11.088
And that is why it is important to deal with them at each age.

192
00:38:11.088 --> 00:38:20.734
The next stage, the warrior stage, the gibbor stage, 20 to 40. This is characterized by lots of energy and motivation, masculine drive.

193
00:38:20.734 --> 00:38:30.523
The goal here is to establish myself, to build something, to prove something to the world. And so in this stage, you see a lot of energy.

194
00:38:30.523 --> 00:38:46.171
In fact, Proverbs 20 verse 29 says, "The glory of young men is their strength." The glory of young men is their strength. Here is the challenge. The challenge is, will I develop a road map? Will I seek to follow a plan?

195
00:38:46.171 --> 00:38:51.673
Do I have a plan to move me forward? Because this stage is life in the fight.

196
00:38:51.673 --> 00:39:05.678
And what you realize is as much energy and as much drive and as much optimism and much go for the glory that you might have in this stage of your life, the guy next to you is doing the same thing.

197
00:39:05.678 --> 00:39:14.752
And so this is a time of heavy competition and not much comradeship. And you need comradeship. You need a plan to move forward.

198
00:39:14.752 --> 00:39:25.470
Unless you are a history buff, you probably do not realize that Napoleon marched for Moscow with 650,000 troops, the largest army amassed in that era.

199
00:39:25.470 --> 00:39:39.832
But he did not have a plan for what he was going to do and how he was going to get home. He came home with somewhere in the neighborhood of 27,000 able-bodied by the time he got home. So what happened?

200
00:39:39.832 --> 00:39:48.978
Well, he got to Moscow. I have been in Red Square. I have seen his cannons. I have been in the Kremlin. I have seen his cannons.

201
00:39:48.978 --> 00:39:59.339
He got there, but he did not have much of a plan for what he was going to do when he got there and what he was going to do when he got back home.

202
00:39:59.339 --> 00:40:06.270
Alexander the Great at age 32 is dying in his tent because there are no more kingdoms to conquer.

203
00:40:06.270 --> 00:40:15.988
Like you went out to conquer but he did not have a plan for his life, did not have a plan for what he was going to do with the conquest.

204
00:40:15.988 --> 00:40:37.853
And so this is the phase where we have to wrestle with the question what is my plan what is my map and most of the time we just want to keep on trucking just plow ahead the next thing the next thing the next thing succeed succeed succeed and along the way we are picking up wounds we are picking up wounds.

205
00:40:37.853 --> 00:40:51.786
In high school I played football in high school and had a really interesting deal here. Our inside linebacker was six feet tall and weighed 165 pounds. And we ran a stunting defense all the time.

206
00:40:51.786 --> 00:41:06.434
But it was unusual to see a guy that was that small playing inside linebacker. But he had two things going for him. The first one is he was faster than lightning. The second one is he was absolutely crazy.

207
00:41:06.434 --> 00:41:09.435
Like crazy, insane, like just create pain.

208
00:41:09.435 --> 00:41:37.874
I remember on one stunt, I played defensive end, so I would come across line and I turn to protect the outside and I am starting to head toward the inside and he comes flying through the line of scrimmage and the quarterback is just handing off the ball to the running back and Rex is actually in projectile like he has left the ground from a full sprint and he is flying through the air like that.

209
00:41:37.874 --> 00:41:53.308
He hits the running back. The ball goes flying and the running back lets out this blood-curdling scream as he goes to the ground. Well, they recover the ball. On the next play, the coach called in the defensive play.

210
00:41:53.308 --> 00:42:06.455
Rex was the captain. He told us what the play was. And as everybody started to line up, he turned to me and I looked at him and there is nothing here. Like just completely glazed over.

211
00:42:06.455 --> 00:42:21.960
There is just nothing here whatsoever. And he said, where do I go? And I said, Rex, you go through that hole right there. He played the rest of the game. Had no idea where he was. Now today, we call that a concussion.

212
00:42:21.960 --> 00:42:28.176
And I guess he did not have enough of those because he is actually an anesthesiologist.

213
00:42:28.176 --> 00:42:38.251
But if you go to him in Wisconsin, you might, if he is irritated with you when he is giving you the drugs, maybe he does have that condition.

214
00:42:38.251 --> 00:42:49.326
But anyway, the deal was he was playing the game and he was picking up wounds along the way. But he was playing so hard he did not even know of the wounds.

215
00:42:49.326 --> 00:43:03.617
And during the stage from 20 to 40, if you just plow ahead without a plan, friction in your marriage, you are picking up wounds from that. Friction with your kids, you are picking up wounds from that.

216
00:43:03.617 --> 00:43:17.622
A deal goes south with a business partner, you are picking up wounds from that. But you are not going to stop. You just keep going and you keep going, which sets you up for the next phase of life.

217
00:43:17.622 --> 00:43:32.270
The next phase of life, the enosh phase, is the wounded stage. It is the wounded stage. It is that 40s and 50s when you begin the powerful re-evaluations and you are wrestling with are the wounds worth it.

218
00:43:32.270 --> 00:43:45.560
Is it really worth it to keep running hard and keep plowing forward? And so the challenge at this phase is am I going to change or am I going to live in Woundedville the rest of my life?

219
00:43:45.560 --> 00:44:00.637
Am I going to make a transition here and really begin to evaluate what my priorities are and what is important to me? Am I going to deal with the hurts of the past? Am I going to deal with the people in my life?

220
00:44:00.637 --> 00:44:15.785
So, the woundedness in my marriage, am I going to lean into that? Start working on it with my kids. Am I going to invest more time in them? Start working on that? Am I going to develop a healthy approach to life?

221
00:44:15.785 --> 00:44:23.716
Or am I just going to say, oh, that is just the way it is. And just keep plowing forward in this stage of life.

222
00:44:23.716 --> 00:44:34.077
The next stage of life, the mature stage, the ish stage, is one of the most meaningful stages of life. One of the most meaningful stages of life.

223
00:44:34.077 --> 00:44:48.582
If you have dealt by age 50 with each of these challenges of the past and put them in its proper perspective and you really have dealt with them, this can be the time when you have the most deep meaning.

224
00:44:48.582 --> 00:45:02.087
Typically, you are less insecure at this point in time. Your relationships have been stabilized. You are pretty confident in what you are good at. You have got your sex drive under control.

225
00:45:02.087 --> 00:45:16.878
You have put that behind you hopefully years before, but you have got that behind you and you start to see a deeper meaning. Read Psalm 23 sometime through the perspective of David at this stage of his life.

226
00:45:16.878 --> 00:45:22.880
And you will see there not a warrior that is going out to try to conquer everything.

227
00:45:22.880 --> 00:45:33.955
You will see there not a hurt man who has had all these conflicts, but a person at rest recognizing the value and the contributions that his life has made.

228
00:45:33.955 --> 00:45:45.745
Here is an opportunity to make strategic moves toward mentoring and investing in people and beginning to shape yourself for the final stage, the zaken stage 60 plus.

229
00:45:45.745 --> 00:45:59.178
Proverbs 20:29 says, "The glory of young men is their strength. Gray hair the splendor of the old." And every one of those gray hairs is a life experience with something valuable to share.

230
00:45:59.178 --> 00:46:13.040
What we need from the 60 plus people, we need standard-bearers. There is nobody in our culture anymore that is raising a standard for manhood and saying, let me show you what life can look like.

231
00:46:13.040 --> 00:46:26.402
Let me walk you through my failures, my successes, and God's work in me. Let me show you what that looks like. Special contributions. Some of the greatest contributions are made after 60.

232
00:46:26.402 --> 00:46:39.835
Moses started his ministry at age 80. Abraham started his journey at age 75. There are all kinds of things that you can do in using your strength and your wisdom and then leaving a legacy.

233
00:46:39.835 --> 00:46:43.264
Who are the younger men that I am building into?

234
00:46:43.264 --> 00:46:56.626
Let me leave you with a letter. So, Robert Lewis, who developed this study and wrote the book, *Raising a Modern-Day Knight*, received a letter. It was from a guy in Saskatchewan, Canada.

235
00:46:56.626 --> 00:47:10.274
He wrote this letter on a typewriter. Anybody remember what those are? 1920s typewriter, not an IBM Selectric with the automatic erase and backspace. It is out of line. There is no backspace.

236
00:47:10.274 --> 00:47:23.350
But when you listen to the letter, you can see the man is still working, still dreaming, still envisioning, still holding up the standard, still leaving a legacy. He writes, "Dear Dr.

237
00:47:23.350 --> 00:47:33.782
Lewis, I am a retired minister of the Mennonite Brethren Church. I am 91 years old. I am now two and a half years in a care home. I have diabetes.

238
00:47:33.782 --> 00:47:45.643
I have heart trouble, but I am able to read and write." Okay, I got these limitations, but I figured out what I can still do, and I am doing it. I can read and write.

239
00:47:45.643 --> 00:47:59.434
"I am in the process now of writing a book for my grandchildren about how to live the Christian life," leaving a legacy. "My wife and I have read your book. We are very pleased to have read it.

240
00:47:59.434 --> 00:48:13.367
We have a family of 13 children. They have all accepted Jesus as their Savior. Some as young children, others as teenagers. They have all married Christian spouses for which we are very thankful.

241
00:48:13.367 --> 00:48:22.299
Of my children, we have three Bible college teachers, six pastors. The rest of our children are serving the Lord in churches.

242
00:48:22.299 --> 00:48:38.019
Our son Daniel Block is the professor of Old Testament interpretation at Southern Baptist Seminary. Dr. Walter Kaiser is his friend. And I am writing to ask permission to use some of your quotes in the book I am writing.

243
00:48:38.019 --> 00:48:48.022
And I would like to leave it as principles that would strengthen my children and grandchildren. I would be pleased to have a reply from you.

244
00:48:48.022 --> 00:48:59.240
Isaac Block." And his handwritten signature is such that it is hardly legible on the page with a shaking 91-year-old hand. You can hardly read his signature.

245
00:48:59.240 --> 00:49:07.385
Here is a guy at 91 living in a nursing home writing a book to his grandkids about how to live the Christian life.

246
00:49:07.385 --> 00:49:12.887
My prayer for all of you, every single one of you, is that that would be you.

247
00:49:12.887 --> 00:49:29.250
Wherever this journey takes you, you would see that by being a man who follows God into your masculinity and deals with the challenges of life along the way that you would be thinking about the legacy that you are leaving behind.

248
00:49:29.250 --> 00:49:36.000
And we will spend more time talking about that next week. So Nate, let me turn it over to you.
